.TH "NIAC" "1" "Sep 2026" "NIAC" "NIAC Manual"

.SH NAME
niac-neighbors - Display neighbor discovery table from LLDP/CDP protocols


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBniac neighbors [watch] [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Display the neighbor discovery table from a running NIAC simulation.

.PP
This command shows network neighbors discovered via LLDP (Link Layer Discovery
Protocol) and CDP (Cisco Discovery Protocol) from simulated devices.

.PP
The table displays:
  - Device:    Local device name
  - Interface: Local interface where neighbor was discovered
  - Neighbor:  Remote device name/chassis ID
  - Protocol:  Discovery protocol (LLDP, CDP, EDP, FDP)
  - LastSeen:  When the neighbor was last seen

.PP
Without arguments, shows the current neighbor table snapshot.
Use the 'watch' subcommand for continuous live updates.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB--api\fP=""
	Daemon API address (default: https://127.0.0.1:8445, or NIAC_API_URL)

.PP
\fB--cacert\fP=""
	Daemon certificate to trust (default: the local daemon's own, when visible)

.PP
\fB--device\fP=""
	Filter by device name

.PP
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for neighbors

.PP
\fB--insecure\fP[=false]
	Skip TLS verification, for a daemon whose certificate this host cannot see

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	Output in JSON format

.PP
\fB--protocol\fP="all"
	Filter by protocol: lldp, cdp, or all


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--storage-keep\fP=500
	Run history records to keep, pruned oldest first on start (0 keeps every run)

.PP
\fB--storage-path\fP=""
	Path to NIAC run history database (default: ~/.niac/niac.db)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  # Show current neighbors table
  niac neighbors

  # Show neighbors in JSON format
  niac neighbors --json

  # Filter by device
  niac neighbors --device router-1

  # Filter by protocol
  niac neighbors --protocol lldp

  # Watch for live updates
  niac neighbors watch

  # Watch with filters
  niac neighbors watch --device switch-1 --protocol cdp

  # Read from a daemon on another address
  niac neighbors --api https://10.0.0.5:8445
.EE


